Below is a minimal “call‑out” you’d put into your test harness or integration script so that Spring’s MVC machinery will invoke the **PersonController**’s *personal* handler and render the correct JSP/Thymeleaf template.

```javascript
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1 – Prepare the inputs.
//   • `webModel`      : the org.springframework.ui.Model instance that
//                        will receive all the attributes needed by the view.
//   • `userRequest`   : the javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest whose
//                        query / form data contains the key/value pair:
//                          username=john_doe
//
// In this toy environment those objects are represented simply as strings
// because the real servlet container isn’t running here.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
await functions.PersonController_personal({
    model:     "webModel",          // <-- actual Model instance at runtime
    request:   "userRequest"        // <-- actual HttpServletRequest instance
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2 – Inspect the return value.
// The controller returns the logical view name (e.g., "personInfo").
// Your front‑end framework will resolve that to a physical file such as
// `/WEB-INF/views/personInfo.jsp` or `/templates/personal.html`.
//
```

### What the controller does under the hood

| Action | Typical implementation |
|--------|-----------------------|
| Read the `username` from the request | `String uname = request.getParameter("username");` |
| Load the corresponding domain entity (`User`, `Profile`, …) via a service/repository layer. |
| Put the retrieved fields onto the `model`: <br>`model.addAttribute("name", profile.getName());<br>model.addAttribute("email", profile.getEmail());` |
| Return the logical view name, e.g.: `<code>"personal"</code>` |

When the above helper runs successfully, the response sent back to the browser will contain whatever markup the `"personal"` view renders—typically a table or card showing John Doe’s full name, email address, phone number, etc.

---

#### Quick sanity check

If you run the snippet in a Node.js–style REPL that has access to the `functions` module, you should see something along the lines of:

```
{ viewName: "personal", statusCode: 200 }
```

(Your exact payload may differ depending on how the wrapper around the Java method formats its reply.)

That’s all you need to trigger the personal‑information page for a given user!

